WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.160
Narrator: Did you know the condom

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:03.760
is over 5,000 years old?

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.300
That's right, some of the
first forms of birth control

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:09.110
date back to around 3,000 BC,

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:11.040
and while the condom has made some strides

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.410
since the Bronze Age, men still don't have

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:16.280
a much better option all
these millennia later.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:18.070
Besides a vasectomy.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:19.860
Especially compared to the pills,

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.069
IUDs, and implants
available to women today.

00:00:23.069 --> 00:00:26.323
So why don't we have
birth control for men?

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.030
In 2002, researchers asked

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.840
more than 9,000 men across four continents

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.920
whether they'd be willing
to use contraception

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.630
capable of preventing sperm production.

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:40.930
Over half said yes.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:43.640
So the problem isn't lack of interest,

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:46.432
it's partly human biology.

00:00:46.432 --> 00:00:49.670
Women ovulate just one
or two eggs each month.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.840
Men, on the other hand,
produce sperm daily,

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.069
and it's not just one or two.

00:00:55.069 --> 00:00:58.520
There's literally hundreds
of millions of sperm

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:02.800
produced each day, so
because there's so many

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.674
sperm produced, actually, you
can reduce your sperm number

00:01:07.674 --> 00:01:12.674
over 90% and still be completely fertile.

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:15.130
Narrator: To reach infertility,
a man's sperm count

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:17.520
needs to be somewhere
around 1 to 10 million

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.230
per milliliter, but getting
there is near impossible,

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:23.430
at least without side effects.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:24.860
That's because sperm count is tied

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:26.743
to the production of testosterone.

00:01:26.743 --> 00:01:30.010
In the past, researchers
tried decreasing testosterone

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:31.860
in an effort to decrease sperm count.

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:36.100
The problem is you don't have any libido,

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.170
you have very little testosterone
to act on other tissues

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:42.680
and so forth, and so the
side effects were so dramatic

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.030
that it really wasn't ever

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:46.370
going to be a contraceptive pill.

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:48.830
Narrator: Scientists also
tried using different compounds

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:51.190
that attack the cells that produce sperm.

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:54.050
But again, biology got in the way.

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:56.910
Germ cells, as they're
called, developed inside

00:01:56.910 --> 00:02:00.250
a fortress-like structure
within the testes.

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:03.150
So literally, nothing can get through it.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:06.940
There's been a lot of
small molecule studies

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:09.070
to try and actually attack the germ cell

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:10.290
to stop it from working.

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:13.850
Literally, I can think of
10 or 15 different compounds

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:16.230
that actually have been
developed to do that,

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:19.260
but they don't work
because of that barrier.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:20.740
Narrator: But the complex male anatomy

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:25.520
isn't the only problem. It's
also funding or lack thereof.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:28.730
In 2002, two big pharmaceutical companies

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:32.980
took interest in male
contraception, Schering and Organon.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:35.850
And together they funded
a large clinical trial

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:38.150
on a hormone-based contraceptive,

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:41.240
offering hope that a
pill backed by Big Pharma

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:43.170
might be on the horizon.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:47.580
Then these two companies
became, as you know,

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:52.190
acquired by bigger company,
and then even bigger company,

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:55.245
so now they are merged in huge companies,

00:02:55.245 --> 00:02:58.675
and women's health is still a priority

00:02:58.675 --> 00:03:01.120
in many of the companies,

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.100
but men's health became part

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:05.750
of the general matter of health.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:08.780
And therefore, the
development of contraception

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:12.010
becomes a really very low priority.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:13.440
Narrator: According to Dr. Wang,

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:15.550
male contraception was also too risky

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:17.479
for Big Pharma at the time.

00:03:17.479 --> 00:03:20.190
The long-term side effects were unknown.

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:22.759
Companies were concerned that
women might not trust it,

00:03:22.759 --> 00:03:26.020
and despite the survey results,
it was unclear whether men

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:29.260
would actually use a
hormone-based contraception.

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:31.420
Today, the limited
funding comes mostly from

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:35.040
government agencies like the
National Institutes of Health.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.530
But there are in fact some
promising lines of research.

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:41.240
Dr. Wang is working on a gel
that can lower testosterone

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:44.670
where it matters, in the
testes where sperm is produced,

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:47.640
while keeping testosterone
levels normal elsewhere.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.610
That means low sperm count
and, more importantly,

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:52.645
no major side effects.

00:03:52.645 --> 00:03:55.030
We have preliminary studies to show

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:58.900
that if we give the gel and
if the man applies the gel,

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:02.770
90% of the men will reach the
level that you talk about,

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:04.450
1 million per mil.

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:06.670
And Skinner is pursuing a new approach,

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:08.220
shutting down Sertoli cells,

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:10.560
which are a part of that
impenetrable barrier

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:11.810
that houses germ cells.

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:14.240
So if you shut down the Sertoli cell,

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:16.420
then you shut down the sperm production.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:17.910
Narrator: But perhaps most promising

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:21.070
is a sort of reversible
vasectomy that's in the works.

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:24.310
So they have this ability
to inject this gel

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:28.350
into what's called the vas deferens,

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:31.230
and it makes this plug,
so then essentially

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:34.040
it does the same thing,
but you're not cutting it.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:36.700
Then believe it or not, you can actually

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:40.630
inject this chemical mixture,
which will dissolve the plug,

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:43.010
and so then you can get
your fertility back.

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:46.120
Narrator: But as promising
as these approaches may be,

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:49.600
they're still years, if not
decades, out, Skinner says.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:51.510
And without more funding, some of them

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:53.290
may never hit the market.

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:57.160
So at least for now, men
are left with few options.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:01.042
Irreversible vasectomies, pulling out ,

00:05:01.042 --> 00:05:05.103
and that slightly updated
Bronze Age invention.

